/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let the phase voltages be va = Vm sin θ, vb = Vm sin(θ − 120°) and vc = Vm sin(θ − 240°), where Vm is the phase peak in volts, Vph = Vm/√2 the phase RMS and VL = √3 Vph the line RMS.
   - The natural firing point is no longer the zero crossing.
   - Three thyristors, one per phase, with their cathodes joined; the load returns to the star point of the transformer.
   - valid for continuous current.
   - The neutral carries the DC load current, so the transformer core sees a DC flux; a zig-zag secondary avoids it.
   - Six thyristors: T1, T3, T5 on phases a, b, c form the upper (common-cathode) group, T4, T6, T2 the lower (common-anode) group.
   - valid for continuous current; Vm is the phase peak, VL the line RMS and Vph the phase RMS, in V.
   - Vdc is 1.35 VL at α = 0, zero at 90° and negative beyond, which needs a DC source to keep the current going, exactly as in the single-phase bridge.
   - The half-controlled three-phase bridge uses three thyristors and three diodes: Vdc = (3√2 VL/2π)(1 + cos α), never negative, with freewheeling built in.
*/
